[8.01, "o", "> Can I fly a K-2 Sparrow at night at 100 m?\r\n  No. Night operations for the K-2 Sparrow limit maximum altitude to 90 meters above ground. At 100 meters, you would be over the allowed night altitude. Also, night flights require form NW-17 signed by the shift lead before the first launch of the night. So to fly at night you must stay at or below 90 m and have the NW-17 form in place. (kes"]
[10.01, "o", "trel_operations_handbook#1)\r\n\r\n> My payload was lost in August. How quickly will someone call me, and what do I get?\r\n  Here’s what happens if your payload was lost (KX-2):\r\n\r\n- Who will call you and when:\r\n  - Customer service will contact you within 4 hours after the incident (KX-2) (kestrel_customer_service_policy#1).\r\n  - The Operations team will be escalated quickly; the handbook notes escalation to the Operations Manager + Customer Service within 1 hour (kestrel_operations_handbook#p1t1r1).\r\n\r\n- What you get:\r\n  - You’ll receive a full refund of the delivery fee for the lost payload, plus a EUR 15 voucher automatically (kestrel_customer_service_policy#0).\r\n  - Refunds are issued automatically within 5 business days (kestrel_customer_service_policy#0).\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- Incident code KX-2 specifically means payload lost or damaged and triggers these steps (kestrel_operations_handbook#p1t1r1). \r\n- Proof and logs related to the incident are retained per policy, if you need any documentation (kestrel_"]
[10.01, "o", "customer_service_policy#1).\r\n\r\n"]
